1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,504
The saved Week 9 reading table has eleven rows. Each row has a book or
source, chapter/page locator, learning outcome, and focus. This guide
keeps those rows separate rather than merging similar-looking titles.

2
00:00:15,504 --> 00:00:28,512
A bilingual explanation is a study aid, not a replacement for the
assigned pages. When the saved school edition cannot be matched to a
local candidate text, the mismatch is stated rather than hidden.

3
00:00:28,512 --> 00:00:41,568
The week joins two explicitly allocated modules, 5B Operational Safety
and 7F Environmental Pathophysiology. Readings support those modules
and the school’s practical stations.

4
00:00:41,568 --> 00:00:56,256
A&P and CV Patho help explain blood flow, oxygen pressure and ECG
effects. MSN, CNS and VSM add assessment and measurement concepts;
they do not all refer to the same edition.

5
00:00:56,256 --> 00:01:08,952
BCEHS and BCPEG do not have the same function. One is the current
service guideline; the other is the school-assigned dated examination
snapshot. Keep both visible when they differ.

6
00:01:08,952 --> 00:01:22,800
FON and PMHN explain stress and grief in a nursing education context.
Use their concepts to support humane communication without diagnosing
a patient or assuming a required emotional response.

7
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:54,600
The recovered AET-folder A and P copy resolves the earlier altitude
page mismatch. Its printed pages 981 to 983 really contain respiratory
adaptation and altitude, followed by the opening of respiratory
development. The full assigned circulation range also includes fetal
pathways and its first key-terms page. The older public candidate
remains a different pagination, not evidence that the actual assigned
pages are missing.

8
00:01:54,600 --> 00:02:17,832
The identified school-folder cardiovascular book has seventy-nine
physical pages, so the assigned pages one hundred eighty-one through
one hundred eighty-three cannot occur in that file. The reader
explains its named calcium and J-wave content with this locator
difference visible. An identified topic is not literal page
equivalence, and no missing page is silently invented.

9
00:02:17,832 --> 00:02:47,784
The recovered CNS, CPSC and PHARM course copies now anchor their
literal assigned spans. In the actual PHARM copy, pages 588 to 592
really contain antiplatelets, and the full assigned Chapter 22 review
includes the later shock-drug material. The earlier candidate
pagination conflict is resolved, not retained as a gap. Nursing-source
treatment examples still do not expand PCP authority.

10
00:02:47,784 --> 00:03:00,072
The saved school outline asks for a 60-second tourniquet competency
and a 30-second load-and-go verbal decision. These are school station
targets, not universal clinical deadlines.

11
00:03:00,072 --> 00:03:18,912
Current BCEHS H09, H10, H12, H02, I01, I02 and N04 provide service-
specific clinical guidance. Use the applicable current pathway,
including its consultation and provider-level requirements.

12
00:03:18,912 --> 00:03:40,200
The lesson now distinguishes the actual March 31, 2025 BCPEG course
copy from the later June 15, 2026 comparison and current service
guidance. Their doses, headings and thresholds are not silently merged
into one action card. The instructor-assessed revision remains a
separate question.

13
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:51,720
For each row, first locate the assigned source. Then identify what the
page teaches and what it does not teach. Page presence alone is not
comprehension.

14
00:03:51,720 --> 00:04:03,984
After reading, explain the concept in your own English words, then
restate it in Chinese while preserving the key English term. This
helps prepare both for English course language and bilingual review.

15
00:04:03,984 --> 00:04:16,608
Finish with a new application question: what would change your
concern, next assessment or escalation? Do not memorize a nursing
action or older protocol as independent PCP authority.

16
00:04:16,608 --> 00:04:33,024
The Kubler-Ross vocabulary is denial, anger, bargaining, depression
and acceptance. Learn the English names for the reading, but do not
turn them into five compulsory steps, a timetable or a grading system
for a family’s response.

17
00:04:33,024 --> 00:04:51,120
The text rejects several unrealistic expectations: everyone following
the same order, finishing by a deadline or expressing emotion in the
same way. Support an individual response and the person’s chosen
connections rather than imposing your own idea of correct grieving.

18
00:04:51,120 --> 00:05:13,008
Emotional effects can include fear, sadness, guilt, regret, anger or
relief, sometimes together. Social effects may include changed roles,
loneliness and strained relationships. None of these observations
proves how much a person cared or establishes a psychiatric diagnosis
on its own.

19
00:05:13,008 --> 00:05:36,264
The reading discusses losses experienced by nurses and the effects of
repeated exposure. For an EMS learner, connect this to fatigue, sleep,
readiness, boundaries and access to confidential support. Burnout,
compassion fatigue and traumatic stress are related concerns, not
interchangeable diagnoses from one difficult shift.

20
00:05:36,264 --> 00:06:10,152
Original rehearsal: following a drowning resuscitation, a partner
repeats the same question and a friend stands silently nearby. The
patient is being transported and the outcome is not yet known. Say
what is confirmed, without predicting death or recovery: the team is
treating the patient and transporting them for further care. Pause,
ask what the partner understood, and ask whom they would like
contacted. Include the chosen friend if the partner wants that
support; silence does not mean the friend is unaffected.

21
00:06:10,152 --> 00:06:44,832
Worked reasoning: repeated questions can reflect difficulty processing
information, so use short statements and check understanding rather
than assigning a denial stage. Offer practical help without promising
a clinical outcome. If either person develops chest pain, breathing
difficulty, collapse or another acute concern, assess that problem on
its own merits. For retrieval, explain one secondary loss, one way
social recognition changes support, and one statement that is both
truthful and compassionate in this case.

22
00:06:44,832 --> 00:07:08,016
For physiology, distinguish two coordinated arms: sympathetic/adrenal
activity contributes to rapid catecholamine responses, while the
hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis regulates cortisol. The book
paragraph compresses these into one chain; its own diagram separates
nerves to the medulla from hormonal signaling to the cortex.

23
00:07:08,016 --> 00:07:30,168
The HPA sequence is hypothalamic CRH, pituitary ACTH and adrenal-
cortex cortisol. NIDDK and the NIOSH review support this distinction
from the sympathetic arm. Do not learn ACTH as a switch that directly
accounts for every sympathetic effect, or infer a hormone
concentration from a pulse rate.

24
00:07:30,168 --> 00:07:51,696
General adaptation syndrome refers here to a whole-body stress
response; local adaptation syndrome concerns a more regional response.
The assigned section names GAS and points to 34.4 for deeper theory.
Do not substitute an extensive unassigned GAS-stage lecture for the
actual local-response content on these pages.

25
00:07:51,696 --> 00:08:12,528
The inflammatory example uses redness, swelling, warmth, pain and loss
of function. A local response can help contain harm, while excessive
or persistent inflammation can also cause problems. These signs do not
by themselves prove infection, and absence of one does not exclude a
serious condition.

26
00:08:12,528 --> 00:08:52,128
Original rehearsal: during a prolonged extrication, a trainee reports
trembling hands, a racing heart and difficulty concentrating. One
teammate calls this adrenal exhaustion. Identify the actual demands
instead: heat, protective equipment, a difficult scene, repeated
alarms, workload and possible inadequate rest. Symptoms alone cannot
establish a hormone level, an exhaustion stage or a psychological
cause. Communicate the difficulty to the supervisor, arrange safe task
coverage and assess any acute illness or injury as indicated.

27
00:08:52,128 --> 00:09:29,544
Worked reasoning: the sympathetic arm and the HPA axis are coordinated
but distinct. The first concerns rapid neural and catecholamine
responses; the second involves hypothalamic CRH, pituitary ACTH and
adrenal-cortex cortisol. Neither can be measured from the trainee's
pulse alone. Name a feasible immediate support and a reassessment,
then a recovery or confidential-support option after the scene. Do not
require the trainee to disclose personal experiences publicly, and do
not use a theory label to certify readiness for a hazardous task.

28
00:09:29,544 --> 00:09:54,120
Anemia illustrates saturation versus oxygen delivery: the available
hemoglobin can be well saturated while its total quantity is
inadequate. Hypovolemia can reduce flow despite a preserved saturation
value. Assess breathing, perfusion, mental status, symptoms and trends
together; SpO2 alone measures neither blood volume nor cardiac output.

29
00:09:54,120 --> 00:10:28,656
Original case: after a cold-water rescue, a patient is pale, breathing
rapidly and becoming confused. A finger probe flickers between ninety-
six and ninety-nine percent while a cuff is cycling on that arm.
Assess the patient immediately; separate the conflicting measurements,
inspect signal quality and use an approved suitable site. Reassess
breathing, circulation, mental status and pressure using a documented
method. A better-looking saturation number does not explain away the
deterioration.

30
00:10:28,656 --> 00:10:57,456
Before an auscultatory reading, estimate systolic pressure by
palpation while inflating until the radial or brachial pulse
disappears. The source teaches a maximum inflation pressure thirty
millimeters of mercury above the disappearance point. This is not
thirty above an assumed normal pressure. A silent auscultatory gap can
otherwise make the later reappearance of sound look like the first
systolic sound, falsely lowering the recorded systolic value.

31
00:10:57,456 --> 00:11:28,512
In the source two-step method, first determine that inflation level
and release the cuff, then position the stethoscope, reinflate to the
determined level and deflate evenly at about two millimeters of
mercury per second. Identify the first Korotkoff sound as systolic and
final disappearance as diastolic, then fully release pressure and
record the result. Use supervised practice and the applicable
measurement procedure; text alone does not establish auscultatory
skill.

32
00:11:28,512 --> 00:11:56,256
Troubleshoot systematically if a brachial pulse or sound is difficult
to find: confirm anatomy, arm position, reasonable palpation pressure,
chestpiece position and selected side, seal, earpiece fit and
surrounding noise. Pressing too hard can obliterate a pulse.
Reposition or use another appropriate method, stating a limitation if
necessary; do not enter a guessed value merely to fill the chart.

33
00:11:56,256 --> 00:12:36,000
Worked retrieval: explain why cold and cuff inflation can degrade the
signal, why saturation is not total oxygen delivery, and why a
preserved systolic pressure cannot exclude hypoperfusion. Then
rehearse choosing the cuff, supporting the arm, estimating inflation
by palpation, identifying sounds and documenting a trend. Compare an
original reading of one hundred twelve over seventy-four with a later
ninety-four over sixty-eight only after checking method and
conditions; their pulse pressures are thirty-eight and twenty-six, but
the patient and trajectory determine concern, not subtraction alone.

34
00:12:36,000 --> 00:13:07,344
The actual page 162 adds culturally safe consent. Explain information
accessibly, avoid coercion, seek consent before a procedure where
feasible and respect the person's ability to withdraw it. Ask whether
an Elder, Knowledge Keeper or chosen advocate would help rather than
assigning a preference from identity. Historical and current power
imbalances are reasons to improve communication and choice, not assume
that an Indigenous patient cannot understand or decide.

35
00:13:07,344 --> 00:13:38,568
The same literal page requires assessment with consent, decision-
specific capacity assessment, discussion of risks and a specific
worsening-symptom plan. A referral must be accepted or follow the
established process, with clear follow-up and documentation; a phone
number alone is not a completed referral. FR autonomous non-conveyance
is excluded, and pediatric or police-custody non-conveyance requires
CliniCall. A signature does not replace the clinical reasoning or a
workable plan.

36
00:13:38,568 --> 00:14:12,384
Original rehearsal: a six-year-old is pulled from a garden pond and is
now awake but coughing. A caregiver wants to leave because the child
is speaking. Establish the event timeline and assess breathing,
oxygenation, temperature and injuries, while explaining why speaking
does not exclude evolving lung injury. Use the pediatric pathway and
required consultation for a non-conveyance decision. Invite the
caregiver's questions and check understanding rather than describing
the request itself as incapacity.

37
00:14:12,384 --> 00:14:49,632
Worked reasoning: water safety comes before contact; then effective
breathing and a complete assessment guide urgency. Explain confirmed
findings and the risk of delayed change, without inventing a prognosis
or guaranteeing that observation will be harmless. Document findings,
advice, consultation, the person's understanding and the actual agreed
disposition. For retrieval, contrast immersion with submersion and
assisted breaths with CPAP, then explain why an A09 page in the
assigned span cannot silently be renamed A08.

38
00:14:49,632 --> 00:15:17,424
Traumatic arrest commonly reflects severe blood loss, inadequate
oxygenation or obstructed circulation. A heart may show organized
electrical activity without enough filling or forward flow to produce
a palpable pulse. Simultaneously addressing hemorrhage, oxygenation
and relevant obstruction targets the mechanism; chest compressions
alone cannot replace missing circulating volume or release a tension
pneumothorax.

39
00:15:17,424 --> 00:15:46,104
The general N04 discussion describes chest decompression, but its
intervention ladder places bilateral needle thoracentesis in ACP care
and additional invasive or blood-product actions at higher levels.
Read a general paragraph together with the role-specific procedure,
not as expanded PCP permission. A team should identify the suspected
threat, request the appropriate resource and avoid delaying other
indicated treatment while waiting.

40
00:15:46,104 --> 00:16:17,304
Page 20 concerns preservation of an amputated part, not initial
tourniquet placement. Stabilize the patient and control life-
threatening bleeding before spending time on the separated tissue.
Preserve the possibility of reattachment without treating the part as
more urgent than breathing, circulation or safe conveyance. The
school's separate sixty-second tourniquet competency measures timely
complete bleeding control and recording, not how quickly the amputated
part is packed.

41
00:16:17,304 --> 00:16:44,472
Do not warm the amputated part, immerse it in water, place it directly
on ice or use dry ice. Distinguish a brief cleaning rinse from leaving
tissue submerged, and indirect cooling through protection from direct
freezing contact. Preserve identification and convey the part with the
patient. Do not attempt reattachment in a classroom rehearsal or delay
urgent patient care while searching indefinitely for missing tissue.

42
00:16:44,472 --> 00:17:17,136
On page 54, hypovolemia means evidence of a significant loss of blood
or body fluid, connected to poor tissue perfusion. The source lists
systolic pressure below ninety and other shock findings, but its
footnote allows use above ninety when the mechanism, injuries or
condition suggest impending shock. Therefore, a still-preserved
pressure does not end the assessment. Identify bleeding or fluid loss,
repeated findings and trajectory rather than waiting for a number to
cross a threshold.

43
00:17:17,136 --> 00:17:48,432
Original arithmetic rehearsal, not a patient order: seventy kilograms
multiplied by twenty milliliters per kilogram equals fourteen hundred
milliliters. The source's five-hundred-milliliter reassessment wording
means that a calculated total is not an uninterrupted automatic
delivery. Explain what findings would change the plan and which
current source authorizes it. A rising pressure alone does not prove
restored tissue perfusion, and a fluid calculation does not replace
hemorrhage control.

44
00:17:48,432 --> 00:18:22,032
The actual 2025 table puts epistaxis and postpartum hemorrhage in a
medical-oversight-consult-required row. The later 2026 table changes
that heading to considerations. That changed label does not itself
grant autonomous administration. The current service monograph
explicitly requires prior CliniCall for its maternal vaginal-
bleeding/postpartum indication; epistaxis is not listed as a current
indication there. Keep the exact source and consultation condition
attached to the use being discussed.

45
00:18:22,032 --> 00:18:56,184
Current service eligibility and exclusions must be read separately:
shock or hypoperfusion with suspected injury-related bleeding is the
trauma context; nontraumatic gastrointestinal bleeding is excluded,
and pediatric age limits differ by provider level. The current
monograph does not list isolated TBI in its contraindication list,
unlike the examination copy, but absence from that list does not
establish an indication when its positive bleeding criteria are
absent. Do not average the two lists or silently substitute one for
the other.

46
00:18:56,184 --> 00:19:35,496
The source box labels cue recognition, pattern recognition, problem
representation, hypothesis generation, hypothesis evaluation and final
judgment as six NCJMM steps. These are not the official six measured
functions. NCSBN names recognize cues, analyze cues, prioritize
hypotheses, generate solutions, take action and evaluate outcomes.
Retain the discrepancy for source-based study instead of memorizing
the box as an exact official sequence. Neither model replaces an
urgent primary assessment or grants nursing or paramedic authority.

47
00:19:35,496 --> 00:20:06,792
Worked reasoning: notice the new pallor, dizziness and reduced
participation; interpret possible impaired perfusion without assuming
one cause; respond with assessment, indicated support and a safer
movement plan; evaluate the response and communicate changes. Keep
these actions within actual scope and device instructions. The
patient's weight-bearing restriction, current assistance needs and
clinical urgency answer three different questions, all of which are
needed before the move.

48
00:20:06,792 --> 00:20:40,008
Brake use is device- and phase-specific. The textbook's blanket
instruction to lock a wheeled lift conflicts with the manufacturer's
Hoyer Advance lifting instruction, which leaves the lift casters
unbraked so it can center under the load. That does not mean every
bed, wheelchair or lift should be unlocked. Identify the exact device
and movement phase, stabilize the receiving surface as required and
follow its complete instructions with training; never combine
fragments into an invented operating sequence.

49
00:20:40,008 --> 00:21:11,304
Distinguish a standard frame, a two-wheeled walker, a four-wheeled
rollator and a forearm-support design. A standard frame is lifted and
positioned before the next supported step; front wheels can allow
advancement without lifting the whole frame. The source paragraph
calls the two wheels rear wheels, but its own figure shows front
wheels, consistent with HealthLinkBC. A rollator's brakes and seat add
their own operating requirements; more wheels do not automatically
mean safer support.

50
00:21:11,304 --> 00:21:45,768
Original rehearsal: a person awaiting movement from a worksite chair
says the injured leg must not bear weight. They use a cane at baseline
but are now pale, dizzy and less able to follow instructions. Do not
use the familiar cane or a previous transfer to justify standing.
Reassess the acute change, protect the injured limb, explain the
revised plan and obtain appropriate equipment and trained help for a
supported move. The route and urgency must reflect the actual
condition, not simply the usual walking aid.

51
00:21:45,768 --> 00:22:18,864
A safe transfer is a planned move, not simply a test of whether
someone can stand. Assess current illness and injuries, mobility,
strength, balance, ability to follow the plan, pain and fatigue, lines
and tubes, weight-bearing restrictions, surfaces, route, equipment and
trained help. A previous successful transfer does not establish
current ability. Connect this to the CPSC assistance-level reading,
while keeping assistance level and permission to load an injured limb
as different questions.

52
00:22:18,864 --> 00:22:49,512
The visceral pleura covers the lung and the parietal pleura lines the
chest wall. Their thin fluid interface normally helps couple lung
movement to chest expansion. Air entering through a damaged chest wall
or visceral pleura disrupts that relationship and permits lung recoil.
Loss of ventilated lung can impair gas exchange. The source's small
normal pleural-fluid volume is an anatomy reference, not a quantity to
aspirate or a measure obtainable from the bedside examination.

53
00:22:49,512 --> 00:23:22,464
Tension is a pressure-and-physiology problem, not merely a synonym for
a large pneumothorax. Progressive intrathoracic pressure can
compromise ventilation and venous return, producing obstructive shock.
A large radiographic air space alone does not establish tension, and a
smaller collection can become dangerous in the wrong physiology.
Tracheal displacement may be late or absent; do not wait for it before
recognizing a deteriorating patient and escalating through the
applicable pathway.

54
00:23:22,464 --> 00:23:57,360
A chest drainage system collects output and limits reverse entry of
air, using a water seal or another manufacturer-specific mechanism.
The illustrated device has a marked collection chamber, an air-
leak/water-seal area, suction control and a bellows indicator. These
parts answer different questions: amount collected, air movement,
selected suction and whether that particular suction mechanism is
working. Identify the actual device and prescribed setup rather than
assuming every drainage unit has the same chambers or controls.

55
00:23:57,360 --> 00:24:36,288
Suction is not automatically required for every chest drain. The
textbook's blanket suction wording should be read against the actual
order and device; selected BTS guidance does not advise routine
initial suction for pneumothorax drainage. Likewise, bubbles must be
interpreted by chamber, pattern, indication and device. Continuous
air-leak bubbling is not made harmless simply because a pneumothorax
was present. Assess the patient and system, preserve the authorized
setup and escalate concerns instead of improvising clamping,
disconnection or suction changes.

56
00:24:36,288 --> 00:25:08,952
Hypovolemic shock is inadequate circulation associated with critical
intravascular volume loss. Real blood loss can be external or
concealed inside the body; the source's description of absolute loss
as visible is too narrow. Vomiting, diarrhea, fistula drainage,
sweating, osmotic diuresis or other excessive urinary losses can also
reduce volume. Identify the actual loss and its mechanism rather than
treating all cases as visible external hemorrhage.

57
00:25:08,952 --> 00:25:39,168
Reduced filling can lower stroke volume and cardiac output.
Sympathetic responses may increase rate, contractility and vascular
tone, temporarily preserving pressure while tissues remain threatened.
Renal and hormonal responses conserve fluid over time. Compensation
has limits and varies with age, medications, illness and injury;
absence of marked tachycardia or hypotension does not exclude serious
hypoperfusion.

58
00:25:39,168 --> 00:26:12,816
Original case: a worker is struck on the left chest by a falling
crate. There is little visible bleeding, but breathing is increasingly
difficult, one side moves less and the worker becomes less responsive
while the measured systolic pressure is still one hundred one. Assess
and support the patient promptly, look for both impaired breathing and
concealed loss, and request the appropriate resources. Do not wait for
tracheal deviation or pressure below ninety, and do not assume that no
visible blood means no volume loss.

59
00:26:12,816 --> 00:26:45,672
Worked reasoning: pleural pressure can obstruct circulation, whereas
bleeding can reduce the amount returning to the heart; both may occur
together after chest trauma. The actual examination and trajectory
determine concern, not one label or normal-looking number. Explain
your findings, likely threats, interventions and response in the
handover. A general textbook procedure or fluid paragraph does not
expand PCP scope, and reassessment continues while definitive care is
arranged.

60
00:26:45,672 --> 00:27:17,136
Begin with the target. Platelet activation and aggregation help build
a plug; coagulation stabilizes it with fibrin. Antiplatelets reduce
platelet activity, anticoagulants interfere with coagulation, and
thrombolytics promote breakdown of fibrin through the enzyme plasmin.
Aspirin is not a medicine that directly dissolves an established clot.
The class name helps interpret a medication history, but it does not
tell you every indication, bleeding risk or reversal plan.

61
00:27:17,136 --> 00:27:53,760
P2Y12 is a platelet receptor for adenosine diphosphate. Clopidogrel
requires metabolic activation, importantly involving CYP2C19; poor
activation can mean less protection, not simply fewer side effects.
Genetic variation and inhibitors such as omeprazole or esomeprazole
can reduce its effect. Opioids can delay absorption of oral P2Y12
drugs. Recognize these history and handover issues without
independently adding a loading dose, changing gastric treatment or
stopping a prescribed antiplatelet.

62
00:27:53,760 --> 00:28:31,056
Assess for visible and concealed bleeding: prolonged wound bleeding,
black stools, blood in urine, coffee-ground vomit, hemoptysis, severe
new headache or deterioration after a head injury. CBC,
hemoglobin/hematocrit trends and platelet counts help in the hospital
context, but a normal early value does not exclude a new bleed.
Platelet number does not directly measure all platelet-function
inhibition. Connect the medication history to injury mechanism,
perfusion, mental status and repeated observations rather than looking
for bruises alone.

63
00:28:31,056 --> 00:29:06,528
Teach both sides of the risk: bleeding needs prompt evaluation, while
premature discontinuation can increase thrombotic events, especially
after coronary intervention. A generic five-to-seven-day preoperative
sentence is not permission for a patient or field provider to choose a
stop date; timing is agent- and procedure-specific and belongs to the
treating team. Explain injury prevention, medication identification
and what changes to report. Use a question such as, What would you do
after a head injury even if the bleeding outside seems small?

64
00:29:06,528 --> 00:29:39,288
Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume, with units
of volume per time. For example, eighty beats per minute times sixty
milliliters per beat equals forty-eight hundred milliliters per
minute, or four point eight liters per minute. That is not the volume
of one beat. The source four-to-five-liter resting reference is not a
normality guarantee for every size, activity or illness. A fast rate
can coexist with poor output if filling and stroke volume fall.

65
00:29:39,288 --> 00:30:15,312
Preload concerns ventricular filling/stretch before contraction,
afterload the load against ejection, and contractility the muscle
response at a given loading state. Venous return influences preload
but is not its interchangeable definition. Pressure can be related
approximately to flow and resistance; it is not a direct ATP meter.
Inadequate oxygen delivery or use can impair cellular energy and
organs. Thus one medicine that raises pressure may not correct the
cause, and excessive afterload can make a failing pump work harder.

66
00:30:15,312 --> 00:30:54,336
Four mechanisms organize shock: loss of effective circulating volume,
failed pumping, obstructed filling/outflow, or inappropriate vascular
distribution/tone. They can coexist. The source wrongly makes reduced
vascular resistance sound like the necessary consequence of blood
loss; sympathetic compensation can instead raise resistance while
stroke volume falls. Distributive shock is not simply all fluid moving
outside vessels. Think about flow, vascular tone, oxygen content and
tissue use together; a preserved cuff pressure does not exclude shock.

67
00:30:54,336 --> 00:31:36,384
Volume loss requires cause control, such as stopping hemorrhage,
alongside appropriate circulatory support and access to definitive
care. Fluid is not equivalent to oxygen-carrying blood or hemostasis.
Pump failure is different: large uncritical fluid loads may worsen
pulmonary congestion. Obstruction is different again: a vasoactive
drug does not remove tension physiology or tamponade. Current D01
calls for judicious fluid use and consultation in suspected
cardiogenic shock. Identify mixed or uncertain shock and escalate
rather than forcing every patient into one drug category.

68
00:31:36,384 --> 00:32:16,080
In anaphylaxis, vasodilation and leakage can impair circulation while
airway swelling or bronchospasm impairs breathing. Epinephrine
addresses several receptor-mediated problems; inhaled salbutamol
addresses bronchospasm, not the whole syndrome. The current E09
initial IM pathway is distinct from advanced refractory IV care and
arrest dosing. Do not delay indicated IM epinephrine for a perfect
monitor setup or replace it with a nebulizer. Observe breathing,
circulation and consciousness in parallel, and communicate whether the
intended response occurs.

69
00:32:16,080 --> 00:32:51,816
Albuterol is the US name used here for salbutamol. Its predominant
beta-two action relaxes bronchial smooth muscle; tremor, tachycardia,
dysrhythmia or paradoxically worse breathing can occur. Other
sympathomimetics, beta blockers and some antidepressants can alter the
response. Listen to air entry and observe work of breathing, rate,
oxygenation and the patient, rather than declaring success because the
nebulizer emptied. New chest pressure, marked palpitations or
worsening distress needs reassessment.

70
00:32:51,816 --> 00:33:31,512
Isotonic refers to effective osmotic behavior relative to plasma, not
a promise that every isotonic solution has exactly the same sodium
concentration as blood. Saline and balanced crystalloid differ in
composition. The source gives rapid one-to-two-liter anaphylaxis and
two-liter hypovolemia examples. Keep those hospital examples visible
as source content, not fixed instructions for every adult, child,
hemorrhage or congested heart. Use the cause-specific current service
pathway, individualized response and overload reassessment; do not
make finishing a bag the clinical goal.

71
00:33:31,512 --> 00:34:13,752
Norepinephrine, also called noradrenaline, stimulates adrenergic
receptors; the prototype table heading alpha/beta antagonist is an
error. Alpha-mediated vasoconstriction and beta-mediated cardiac
effects can support selected hypotension, but the pulse does not
always rise: reflex bradycardia can occur. A higher pressure can
coexist with poor flow when volume is inadequate. The source statement
no contraindications does not mean no precautions. Recognize ischemia,
arrhythmia, excessive vasoconstriction, rebound hypotension after
abrupt cessation and IV extravasation injury.

72
00:34:13,752 --> 00:34:49,296
Original Week 9 case: a maintenance worker falls from a platform and
reports taking two tablets after a coronary stent, but cannot name
them. There is chest pain, a painful swollen thigh, cool skin and
increasing confusion. A coworker reports a recent missed dose. State
the immediate assessment and hemorrhage-control priorities, how to
verify the medicines without delaying care, and what the history adds
to the handover. Neither a possible antiplatelet nor a missed dose
establishes the single cause of deterioration.

73
00:34:49,296 --> 00:35:32,688
Worked reasoning: trauma can cause concealed volume loss, impaired
ventilation or obstruction, while coronary disease remains relevant.
Assess actual breathing, pulse and perfusion; look for accessible
bleeding and other time-critical injuries, arrange resources and
conveyance, and report exact observations and uncertainty. Do not
reflexively give nitrate for chest pain, a hospital vasopressor for
one low pressure, or an extra antiplatelet to compensate for an
unverified missed dose. Change only the later history to a recent head
strike: concern for intracranial bleeding increases even if external
bleeding is minimal.

74
00:35:32,688 --> 00:36:11,280
Trace the pulmonary loop aloud: systemic veins and coronary sinus to
right atrium, tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonary valve and
trunk, right/left pulmonary arteries, smaller branches and alveolar
capillaries. After exchange, pulmonary venules and usually four
pulmonary veins return blood to the left atrium. Pulmonary arteries
therefore normally carry relatively oxygen-poor blood and pulmonary
veins relatively oxygen-rich blood. The bronchial systemic supply
serves lung tissue itself and is not the same route as alveolar gas-
exchange flow.

75
00:36:11,280 --> 00:36:46,824
The usual arch has three major branches: brachiocephalic trunk, left
common carotid and left subclavian. The right common carotid and right
subclavian arise from the brachiocephalic trunk; do not invent a
matching left brachiocephalic artery. Each subclavian supplies
branches including vertebral, internal thoracic and thyrocervical
pathways before continuing toward the arm. In contrast, both right and
left brachiocephalic veins exist. Similar words do not make the
arterial and venous trees mirror images.

76
00:36:46,824 --> 00:37:24,576
For the arm, follow subclavian, axillary, brachial, then radial and
ulnar to palmar arches and digital branches. Radial follows the thumb-
side radius; ulnar follows the little-finger-side ulna in anatomical
position. Humeral circumflex, deep brachial and collateral branches
serve shoulder, posterior arm and elbow regions along the way. The
arches link channels in the hand; their presence does not guarantee an
injured hand has adequate perfusion. This route explains why
assessment above and below an injury answers different questions.

77
00:37:24,576 --> 00:38:01,488
For the leg, follow external iliac, femoral and popliteal, then
anterior tibial to dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial to plantar
supply. Deep femoral/circumflex and genicular branches serve thigh and
knee territories; fibular or peroneal names refer to the same lateral-
leg pathway. Resolve the source ambiguous pronoun with its own table:
medial and lateral plantar arteries arise from posterior tibial, not
from a fibular artery that simply splits into both. Dorsal and plantar
anastomoses distribute blood toward toes.

78
00:38:01,488 --> 00:38:34,752
Upper-body return usually reaches the superior vena cava through the
paired brachiocephalic veins. Each brachiocephalic is formed mainly by
internal jugular plus subclavian; external jugular usually drains into
subclavian, while vertebral and internal thoracic tributaries also
contribute. The two brachiocephalics unite into SVC, which receives
the azygos arch and empties into right atrium. Coronary venous return
through the coronary sinus is a separate route directly to that
atrium.

79
00:38:34,752 --> 00:39:15,096
The hepatic portal route connects two exchange beds before general
venous return: digestive/splenic capillaries, portal tributaries and
vein, liver sinusoids, then hepatic veins and IVC. Superior mesenteric
plus splenic commonly form the portal vein; inferior mesenteric often
joins splenic, while gastric and cystic tributaries contribute.
Hepatic arterial blood also reaches the liver. This arrangement
supports nutrient handling and first-pass processing, but does not
mean every swallowed substance is completely removed or that portal
and systemic veins are interchangeable.

80
00:39:15,096 --> 00:39:47,472
In fetal circulation, the placenta provides gas exchange. One
umbilical vein brings relatively oxygen-rich fetal blood toward the
fetus; two umbilical arteries return relatively oxygen-poor blood
toward placenta. The fetal and maternal circulations exchange across
the placental interface rather than normally forming one continuous
shared blood pipe. Not all oxygenated umbilical blood bypasses the
liver, and the fetal lungs still receive blood for growth even though
they are not the main gas-exchange organ.

81
00:39:47,472 --> 00:40:30,384
Know three shunts and their adult remnants. Foramen ovale permits
right-to-left atrial flow and leaves the fossa ovalis; ductus
arteriosus connects pulmonary trunk toward aorta and leaves ligamentum
arteriosum; ductus venosus links umbilical return toward IVC while
bypassing much hepatic exchange and leaves ligamentum venosum. After
birth, lung expansion and lower pulmonary resistance increase left-
atrial filling relative to right, helping close the atrial flap.
Oxygen and circulatory changes promote ductal closure; functional and
anatomical closure are not all instantaneous with the first breath.

82
00:40:30,384 --> 00:41:21,240
Altitude lowers barometric pressure while the oxygen fraction remains
about twenty-one percent. The source dry-air table pairs pressures of
760, 632, 565, 447, 350 and 260 millimeters of mercury with oxygen
partial pressures about 159, 133, 118, 94, 73 and 54. These examples
span sea level through roughly twenty-nine thousand feet. They are not
arterial blood-gas values or saturation percentages. For example,
twenty-one percent of 760 is about 160, whereas twenty-one percent of
447 is about 94; the fraction stayed similar while the pressure fell.

83
00:41:21,240 --> 00:41:59,832
Adaptation has different time scales. Ventilatory and acid-base
responses occur early; reduced plasma volume can increase hemoglobin
concentration without immediately making new red cells. EPO-mediated
production contributes over longer exposure. Thus an immediate higher
hematocrit is not proof that EPO already produced a new red-cell mass.
Acclimatization reduces risk but is not guaranteed protection at a new
higher sleeping altitude. Hydration supports ordinary needs; forced
drinking is not a substitute for acclimatization, descent or
treatment.

84
00:41:59,832 --> 00:42:34,776
Original integration: at a high camp, a worker cuts a thigh on damaged
equipment and later has increasing breathlessness and confusion. One
finger saturation seems plausible for the altitude. Explain why that
number cannot exclude concealed blood loss, impaired ventilation or
altitude illness, and trace how a fall in venous return can reduce
output. The immediate task is a coordinated assessment, hemorrhage
control, environmental protection and resource/evacuation plan, not
choosing a single reassuring measurement.

85
00:42:34,776 --> 00:43:17,256
Worked explanation: the pulmonary loop loads oxygen, systemic arterial
branches distribute blood, and venous routes return filling volume. A
local injury and a lower inspired oxygen pressure can impair different
parts of this chain simultaneously. Assess the whole patient and
trends while addressing time-critical threats. For retrieval, draw one
arm arterial route, one foot-to-heart venous route, the liver portal
sequence and the three fetal shunts; then explain altitude using
fraction versus partial pressure. The guide covers the assigned
narrative domains and selected figures, not every optional link or
source assessment.
